{:edb/db-before-t 2809 :edb/db-after-t 2810 :edb/tx-data [[13194139536122 50 #inst "2026-10-09T15:39:49.295Z" 13194139536122 true] [17592186124450 1020 #edb/ref 17592186384631 13194139536122 true] [17592186124526 1020 #edb/ref 17592186384617 13194139536122 true] [17592186124526 1020 #edb/ref 17592186384624 13194139536122 true] [17592186125168 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139536122 true] [17592186125168 1100 "If $A$ is a $3 \\cdot 3$ matrix and $nullity(A + 2I) = 2$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $rank(A + 2I) = 2$\n2. $A + 2I$ is singular\n3. $λ =-2$ is an eigenvalue of $A$" 13194139536122 false] [17592186125168 1100 "If $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A+2I)=2,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\text{rank}(A+2I)=2$\n- $A+2I$ is singular\n- $\\lambda =-2$ is an eigenvalue of $A$" 13194139536122 true] [17592186125168 1145 "Given a $3\\times 3$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=3=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $M$ has $3$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(M)+\\text{nullity}(M)=3.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. According to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A+2I) & =3-\\text{nullity}(A+2I) \\\\  & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statements II is true. By the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{nullity}(A+2I)\\ne 0,$ we obtain that $A+2I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement III is true. Notice that if $M=A+2I$ is singular, then $(A+2I)x=0$ has nonzero solutions which will be eigenvectors of $A.$ As a result, $\\lambda =-2$ is an eigenvalue of $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139536122 true] [17592186125174 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139536122 true] [17592186125174 1100 "If $A$ is a $2 \\cdot 2$ matrix and $λ =-1$ is an eigenvalue of $A$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $A + I$ is a non-singular matrix\n2. $Col(A + I) = R^{2}$\n3. $Null(A + I) \\ne 0$" 13194139536122 false] [17592186125174 1100 "If $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $\\lambda =-1$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A+I$ is a non-singular matrix\n- $\\text{Col}(A+I)={R}^{2}$\n- $\\text{Null}(A+I)\\ne 0$" 13194139536122 true] [17592186125174 1145 "Given a $2\\times 2$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{2} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=2=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nLet's now apply the theorem to the matrix $M=A+I.$\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that if $\\lambda =-1$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ then the nonzero solutions of $(A+I)x=0$ are eigenvectors of $A.$ As a result, the matrix $M=A+I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statements II is false. Since $M=A+I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{Col}(A+I)\\ne {R}^{2}.\n$$\n\n- Statements III is true. Since $M=A+I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A+I)\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139536122 true] [17592186384617 1017 #edb/ref 17592186384618 13194139536122 true] [17592186384617 1089 "q-97897" 13194139536122 true] [17592186384617 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139536122 true] [17592186384617 1100 "If $B$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $\\lambda =0$ is an eigenvalue of $B,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $Bx=0$ has only zero solution\n- $\\text{Col}(B)\\ne {R}^{2}$\n- $\\text{nullity}(B)=0$" 13194139536122 true] [17592186384617 1113 #uuid "7bae13f0-ed61-5405-af6c-4709e99174f3" 13194139536122 true] [17592186384617 1145 "Given a $2\\times 2$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{2} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=2=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nLet's now apply the theorem to the matrix $M=B-0I=B.$\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that if $\\lambda =0$ is an eigenvalue of $B,$ then the nonzero solutions of $(B-0I)x=0$ are eigenvectors of $B.$ As a result, the matrix $M=B$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $M=B$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{Col}(B)\\ne {R}^{2}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $M=B$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(B)\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139536122 true] [17592186384618 1000 #edb/ref 17592186384619 13194139536122 true] [17592186384618 1000 #edb/ref 17592186384620 13194139536122 true] [17592186384618 1000 #edb/ref 17592186384621 13194139536122 true] [17592186384618 1000 #edb/ref 17592186384622 13194139536122 true] [17592186384618 1000 #edb/ref 17592186384623 13194139536122 true] [17592186384618 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139536122 true] [17592186384618 1041 "selection" 13194139536122 true] [17592186384618 1104 #edb/ref 17592186384623 13194139536122 true] [17592186384618 1110 #uuid "a3c78716-464b-5ab8-bd64-0dcbe92d55db" 13194139536122 true] [17592186384619 1034 "II and III only" 13194139536122 true] [17592186384619 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139536122 true] [17592186384619 1111 #uuid "6c2daa22-1727-5a47-850e-1f9e36cb83d0" 13194139536122 true] [17592186384620 1034 "I only" 13194139536122 true] [17592186384620 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139536122 true] [17592186384620 1111 #uuid "650d3df9-0dfc-5d9d-95d6-53e957a7bad7" 13194139536122 true] [17592186384621 1034 "III only" 1319413953